WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.650
Tim Burton brings his vision of a&nbsp;
dark Dreamland to the big screen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.060
a live-action remake of Dumbo, but what did&nbsp;
he change from the original movie and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.380
does it compare to the animated classic?
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.660
today I'm explaining the top twelve biggest&nbsp;
differences between Disney's 1941 Dumbo movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.010
and the brand new live-action Dumbo film.
Plus, I'll be revealing some easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.660
to the original movie you may have missed.
One of the biggest changes in the live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.460
movie is that there are no talking animals.
That means that Dumbo's best friend Timothy Q&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.690
Mouse is no longer part of the story, though&nbsp;
we do get a nod to him as two of the movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:41.130
new characters, Milly and Joe, have a little&nbsp;
mouse circus and not only is one of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:45.990
mice dressed like the animated rodent, but we&nbsp;
even see it eat a peanut, just like the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:51.150
time we met Timothy in the classic film.
Milly and Joe, in fact, take on Timothy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:56.100
role as Dumbo's confidant and champion; so&nbsp;
they stand up for Dumbo when he's mistreated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.340
comfort him when his mother's locked&nbsp;
up, and help him with his flying.
&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.380
In keeping with the filmmakers' desire to&nbsp;
make the world feel as real as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.540
Dumbo's mother, Mrs Jumbo, is actually pregnant&nbsp;
with her baby in the live-action remake, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.530
there aren't any/are no baby-delivering storks,&nbsp;
though there is an easter egg to their animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:18.570
role as we see them in the new movie just before&nbsp;
we glimpse baby Dumbo for the very first time.
&nbsp;

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:23.730
Like in the original, Mrs Jumbo protects her&nbsp;
young son from bullying and threats, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:28.860
once again lands her in elephant jail, though&nbsp;
on this occasion, the filmmakers up the stakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:34.740
making the consequences for shielding her son even&nbsp;
more dire, though they also add a modern twist to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.840
her eventual reunion with her little one.
There's no chorus of female elephants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.890
criticise and ridicule Dumbo this time,&nbsp;
but he does get more than enough of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.660
from some of the circus workers, crowd, and&nbsp;
even Danny DeVito's Ringmaster at first.
&nbsp;

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.420
Talking storks, elephants, and mice aren't the&nbsp;
only creatures cut from the live-action film.
&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.820
Also canned are the Crows who helped young Dumbo&nbsp;
fly, and their use of feathers and psychology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.390
to encourage his aerial abilities&nbsp;
is taken over by Joe and Milly.
&nbsp;

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:08.430
The Crows' absence from the new movie is hardly&nbsp;
surprising given they've been heavily criticised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:13.060
as racist stereotypes of African Americans.
There is a little easter egg to their famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.440
song "When I See An Elephant Fly" though&nbsp;
when the Dreamland ringmaster introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.919
Dumbo's act with a slight variation on&nbsp;
the following lines from the Crows:
&nbsp;

00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:20.978
"Well, I've seen a horsefly."
"Ah, I've seen a dragonfly."
&nbsp;

00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:28.780
"Yeah, I've seen a housefly."
Most of the live-action film's new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.980
characters are human, but there is one&nbsp;
notable new animal addition: a monkey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.790
who just like the other animals never talks&nbsp;
and is completely computer generated.
&nbsp;

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:42.640
The new CGI primate reminds me of the popular&nbsp;
real-life one that featured in the Night at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.710
Museum movies; and, like that capuchin monkey,&nbsp;
this new mischievous monkey's role is to add some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.010
comic relief, which it does plenty of by popping&nbsp;
up in some surprising places and by performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.150
both Shakespeare and damsel-in-distress&nbsp;
routines as part of the circus and also by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.570
popping up in some surprising places.
"Is that a monkey in your desk?"
&nbsp;

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:02.590
"Just for emergencies."
Just like the star of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.540
animated movie, the computer-generated lead&nbsp;
of the live-action film is entirely adorable.
&nbsp;

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.630
And despite the many tweaks to&nbsp;
the airborne elephant's story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.880
such as the origin of his name and how we find&nbsp;
out he can fly, Dumbo's character remains as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.590
heart-warming and moving as before, which means&nbsp;
his adventures will likely break your heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.990
before finally putting it back together again.
And, the film's core messages of self-belief and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:30.370
that difference is strength not weakness are&nbsp;
still very much there; indeed, they're further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:37.060
highlighted by Dumbo's adopted human family.
The classic movie lasts a mere 64 minutes compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.190
to the new film's almost two-hour runtime, so it's&nbsp;
hardly surprising there's plenty of new material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:47.020
in Tim Burton's reimagining of the tale. 
In fact, the scene where Dumbo flies down from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.610
burning building at the end of the original film&nbsp;
is recreated about a third of the way into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:56.170
live-action movie and is really the jumping-off&nbsp;
point for a whole new strand to the flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:01.210
elephant's story, which is now set just after&nbsp;
the First World War rather than during the Second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:06.340
World War when the animated film was made.
In another change, this time Dumbo doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.160
get drunk on champagne and&nbsp;
hallucinate pink elephants.
&nbsp;

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.630
However, there is a gorgeous version of that&nbsp;
memorable moment as the circus performers make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.650
bubble elephants that multiply and dance.
There's even a suggestion that the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.700
baby Dumbo gets mesmerised watching the&nbsp;
performance as he nods along to the music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.740
with an entranced look upon his face.
When it comes to songs, Dumbo takes a leaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.210
out of Disney's live-action Cinderella rather&nbsp;
than their recent take on Beauty And The Beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.230
with only one of the full songs from the&nbsp;
animation making it into the new film.
&nbsp;

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:39.760
Actress Sharon Rooney, who plays a circus&nbsp;
mermaid, gives a sweet performance of "Baby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.230
Mine", the beautiful lullaby that accompanies an&nbsp;
emotional scene between Dumbo and his mother.
&nbsp;

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:49.090
And you should listen out for some nice easter&nbsp;
eggs to the animation's other famous songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.870
including a nod to "Pink Elephants&nbsp;
on Parade" as well as Danny DeVito&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:57.130
singing a couple of lines from "Casey Jr".
Plus, during the credits, you'll hear different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.990
versions of those songs again as well as a jazzy&nbsp;
instrumental of "When I See An Elephant Fly".
&nbsp;

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.340
With the lack of talking animals,&nbsp;
the human characters in Burton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.720
movie become much more important.
Danny DeVito is great as the struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.730
circus owner, Max Medici, who has a bigger role&nbsp;
in the new film than the original Ringmaster.
&nbsp;

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:21.280
Initially, Medici wants to hide Dumbo's large&nbsp;
ears from the circus-going public, and although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.800
he echoes some of the animated elephants' mean&nbsp;
words and locks up Mrs Jumbo, he turns out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.510
a more obviously sympathetic character than&nbsp;
his animated counterpart, as he tries to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:36.250
the best he can for his large circus family of&nbsp;
strongmen, mermaids, snake charmers, and more.
&nbsp;

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:41.980
The addition of Michael Keaton's V.A. Vandevere is&nbsp;
a major change from the original animation, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:46.360
although the chorus of elephants are incredibly&nbsp;
nasty to Dumbo and the tormenting boys are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:52.510
horribly cruel, there's no one stand-out villain.
In contrast, Keaton's ruthless entrepreneur who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:56.650
desperate to buy Dumbo's circus so he&nbsp;
can exploit the miraculous pachyderm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.580
and make a mountain of money adds a new&nbsp;
and interesting plot to this version.
&nbsp;

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.680
It also takes us to Dreamland, a theme park that&nbsp;
Vandevere sees as the future of entertainment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.030
but which has a much darker side. 
Something else the filmmakers wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.480
to achieve with the human characters&nbsp;
was that Dumbo's story would be a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.970
for them to reflect on their own situations.
So, for example, former circus star and wounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:23.950
war veteran Holt Farrier's relationship with his&nbsp;
children parallels the relationship between Dumbo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:29.560
and Mrs Jumbo because, just as young Dumbo is&nbsp;
trying to reconnect with his mum, so too Holt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:34.430
his children are trying to put their family back&nbsp;
together after being forced apart by the war.
&nbsp;

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:38.510
Milly and Joe also know how it feels to&nbsp;
have lost a mother which adds an extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:43.640
dimension to the bond they form with Dumbo.
Although the human characters are a neat way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.900
the filmmakers to differentiate this version&nbsp;
of Dumbo from the original, they're an area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.370
where the new movie can fall a little flat.
Even though they get more screentime than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.410
their animated counterparts like Timothy Q Mouse,&nbsp;
generally they just don't make the same impact.
&nbsp;

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.630
The issue isn't the cast list, which is suitably&nbsp;
impressive including Colin Farrell, Eva Green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:07.280
and Alan Arkin; the thing is that there are&nbsp;
almost too many new human characters to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.750
point that they're pretty thinly drawn.&nbsp;
Still, this is Dumbo's story and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.980
still a lot of sweetness, tears, and joy to be&nbsp;
found in this beautifully designed world.
